<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_return.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-return">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-return">
  <primary>RETURN</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>RETURN</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>RETURN</refname>
  <refpurpose>end a query and name the columns of its result</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
RETURN [ DISTINCT | ALL ] { * | <replaceable class="parameter">expression</replaceable> [ AS <replaceable class="parameter">alias</replaceable> ] } [, ...]
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-return-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>RETURN</command> is the terminal projection. It evaluates its expressions once
   per row and emits them as the result columns of the query. A read query has to end with
   <command>RETURN</command> or <xref linkend="cypher-finish"/>; a write query may end with
   either of those or with the write clause itself.
  </para>

  <para>
   A column taken from a property comes back as <type>jsonb</type>.
   <literal>RETURN m.title</literal> prints <literal>"The Matrix"</literal>, with the quotes,
   and so does <literal>RETURN m.title::text</literal>. To get a plain scalar, wrap the query
   in SQL. See <xref linkend="cypher-return-notes"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>*</literal> projects every named binding in scope. A pattern element left unnamed
   is not projected. An aggregate in the list groups the rows implicitly: every other
   expression in the list becomes the grouping key, as it would in SQL under a
   <literal>GROUP BY</literal>. <literal>DISTINCT</literal> removes duplicate result rows, and
   <literal>ALL</literal> is the explicit spelling of the default.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>ORDER BY</command>, <command>SKIP</command>, <literal>OFFSET</literal> and
   <command>LIMIT</command> are clauses in their own right that follow a
   <command>RETURN</command>. They are not part of the projection. See
   <xref linkend="cypher-orderby"/>, <xref linkend="cypher-skip"/> and
   <xref linkend="cypher-limit"/>. Unlike <xref linkend="cypher-with"/>,
   <command>RETURN</command> allows the same output name twice, because no later clause has to
   resolve it.
  </para>

 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-return-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>DISTINCT</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Removes duplicate rows from the result.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>ALL</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Keeps duplicate rows. This is the default.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>*</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Projects every named binding currently in scope.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">expression</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Any Cypher expression over the bindings in scope. That includes a vertex or an edge
      variable, a property reference, a path variable, a function call, an aggregate, an
      arithmetic or <literal>CASE</literal> expression, a list or map constructor, and a
      constant. See <xref linkend="cypher-expr"/>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">alias</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The name of the result column. Without double quotes it folds to lower case. Quote it to
      keep the case you wrote. With no alias, a property reference takes the name of the
      property and anything else becomes <literal>?column?</literal>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-return-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Return a whole vertex. A vertex prints as <literal>label[id]{properties}</literal>:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person {name: 'Keanu Reeves'})
RETURN p;

                         p
---------------------------------------------------
 person[4.1]{"born": 1964, "name": "Keanu Reeves"}
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   An edge prints the same way with its two endpoints added:
<programlisting>
MATCH (:person {name: 'Keanu Reeves'})-[r:acted_in]-&gt;(:movie {title: 'The Matrix'})
RETURN r;

                   r
---------------------------------------
 acted_in[5.1][4.1,3.1]{"role": "Neo"}
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   An unaliased property reference takes the name of the property, so
   <literal>RETURN p.born</literal> heads its column <literal>born</literal>. An alias renames
   it, folding to lower case unless it is quoted:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person {name: 'Keanu Reeves'})
RETURN p.born AS "BornIn";

 BornIn
--------
 1964
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>*</literal> returns one column per named element of the pattern. Written
   <literal>-[:acted_in]-&gt;</literal>, without a variable, the edge would not appear:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person {name: 'Ryan Gosling'})-[r:acted_in]-&gt;(m:movie)
RETURN *;

                         p                          |                   r                   |                                                                                                                                     m
----------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 person[4.10]{"born": 1980, "name": "Ryan Gosling"} | acted_in[5.13][4.10,3.9]{"role": "K"} | movie[3.9]{"plot": "A new blade runner unearths a long-buried secret that has the potential to plunge what's left of society into chaos.", "year": 2017, "genre": "Sci-Fi", "title": "Blade Runner 2049", "embedding": [0.21560573, -0.07505179, -0.01331814, 0.13403069]}
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Twenty five <literal>acted_in</literal> edges point at films of three genres, so
   <literal>DISTINCT</literal> cuts the result to three rows:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person)-[:acted_in]-&gt;(m:movie)
RETURN DISTINCT m.genre AS genre
ORDER BY genre;

    genre
-------------
 "Action"
 "Adventure"
 "Sci-Fi"
(3 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   An aggregate with no other expression beside it collapses the whole input to one row.
   List any other expression alongside it and that expression becomes the grouping key:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person)-[:acted_in]-&gt;(m:movie)
RETURN m.genre AS genre, count(*) AS roles
ORDER BY genre;

    genre    | roles
-------------+-------
 "Action"    | 13
 "Adventure" | 1
 "Sci-Fi"    | 11
(3 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>collect()</literal> gathers a group into one list:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person)-[:acted_in]-&gt;(m:movie {title: 'The Matrix'})
RETURN collect(p.name) AS cast;

                            cast
------------------------------------------------------------
 ["Keanu Reeves", "Laurence Fishburne", "Carrie-Anne Moss"]
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-return-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   Because a column taken from a property comes back as <type>jsonb</type>,
   <command>RETURN</command> is a poor interface for an application that wants native types.
   Put the Cypher in a <command>SELECT</command> subquery and unbox it there with the jsonb
   operators: <literal>-&gt;&gt;</literal> reads a key as text, and
   <literal>#&gt;&gt; '{}'</literal> extracts a jsonb scalar column as text:
<programlisting>
SELECT d.props -&gt;&gt; 'title' AS film, r.score
FROM (MATCH (p:person)-[:directed]-&gt;(m:movie)
      WHERE p.name = 'Christopher Nolan'
      RETURN properties(m) AS props) d
JOIN rating r ON r.title = d.props -&gt;&gt; 'title'
WHERE r.source = 'critics'
ORDER BY r.score DESC;

     film     | score
--------------+-------
 Inception    |   8.8
 Interstellar |   8.7
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   An <command>ORDER BY</command> after <literal>RETURN DISTINCT</literal> may sort only by
   expressions that appear in the projection, which is the SQL rule for
   <literal>SELECT DISTINCT</literal>. Project the sort key, or drop
   <literal>DISTINCT</literal> and deduplicate with a grouping aggregate instead:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie) RETURN DISTINCT m.genre AS genre ORDER BY m.year;

ERROR:  for SELECT DISTINCT, ORDER BY expressions must appear in select list
LINE 1: ... (m:movie) RETURN DISTINCT m.genre AS genre ORDER BY m.year;
                                                                ^
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-return-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   <command>RETURN</command> implements the GQL return statement of ISO/IEC 39075:2024,
   section 14.11. The standard gives a result column the declared type of its expression.
   AgensGraph returns every Cypher result column as <type>jsonb</type> whatever the type of
   the expression is, including after an explicit cast. Unbox in SQL, as above.
  </para>

  <para>
   openCypher writes <command>ORDER BY</command>, <command>SKIP</command> and
   <command>LIMIT</command> as trailing modifiers of <command>RETURN</command>. AgensGraph
   accepts that spelling unchanged and also accepts each of the three as a clause of its own
   anywhere in a query. <literal>ALL</literal> after <command>RETURN</command> is the
   standard spelling of the default and is not part of openCypher.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

